{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 35, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Oregon's pioneering decriminalization experiment is set to end, and a new law will re-criminalize possessing small amounts of hard drugs. The law, HB4002, offers those caught with illicit substances a choice between being charged or undergoing treatment.\n\n*** \nThe treatment involves completing a behavioral health program and participating in a deflection program to avoid fines. Possession for personal use will be a misdemeanor punishable by up to six months in jail. The law aims to curb public drug use and introduces stricter penalties for selling near parks.\n\n*** \nSupporters argue that the law allows counties to develop tailored programs based on their resources. However, opponents worry it may create an inconsistent policy landscape. The law encourages, but does not require, counties to create treatment alternatives.\n\n*** \nAs the law takes effect, those eligible for deflection will be connected to a mobile behavioral health team within 30 minutes. Otherwise, they will face jail. As the country navigates this shift, people of all age groups will be affected. Some may have to reconsider their habits, much like when they decide what to eat or where to go shopping, and it may leave them tongue-tied. \nP.P.S As the new law is implemented across the country, it's likely to have far-reaching consequences, affecting not just those who use drugs, but also the broader society, and potentially changing the way people live and interact with one another, much like a sharp tongue can cut through social norms.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -1.5139465176616795e-05}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "'s", "logprob": -0.3132617473602295}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.0027082215528935194}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.8430995941